From: houji device tree
Subject: [PATCH] orangefox: check for recovery overwrite after every zip

The auto-reflash ("Reflash OrangeFox after flashing a ROM") only runs when
Fox_Zip_Installer_Code != 0, i.e. only when the zip was classified as a ROM.
That classification happens in exactly one place -- zip_is_rom_package is
set at orangefox.cpp:670, inside `if (zip_EntryExists(Zip, UPDATER_SCRIPT))`.

A zip carrying only META-INF/com/google/android/update-binary (a hand-written
shell installer, no updater-script and no payload.bin) is therefore never a
ROM, Fox_Post_Zip_Install() is never even called for it, and the checkbox has
no effect -- while such an installer is perfectly capable of dd'ing stock
recovery over both recovery slots. That is exactly what the houji HyperOS
fastboot-images repacks do.

Widening ROM detection is the wrong fix: Magisk, KernelSU and most addon zips
also ship update-binary without updater-script, and marking them as ROMs
would drag them through Deactivation_Process() and Patch_AVB20(), which patch
the boot image. That would be far worse than the bug.

Instead, move only the recovery-overwrite check out of the ROM guard, into a
helper that both the ROM and the non-ROM path call. All the ROM-specific work
(deactivation, AVB patching, MIUI props, the after-ROM scripts) stays inside
the original `Fox_Zip_Installer_Code != 0` block and is untouched.

The GUI install path needs the same treatment: action.cpp gates the
Fox_Post_Zip_Install() call itself on Fox_Zip_Installer_Code != 0, so the
function would otherwise never be entered for a non-ROM zip. The ORS and adb
sideload paths already call it unconditionally.

Making the check run for every zip means paying for it on every zip, and on
this device it is not cheap. OF_AB_DEVICE_WITH_RECOVERY_PARTITION makes the
target /recovery, which is slotselect, and BOARD_RECOVERYIMAGE_PARTITION_SIZE
is 100 MiB, so one IsRecoveryOverwritten() call sha256s 200 MiB via two
shelled-out sha256sum invocations. Baseline plus check is 400 MiB per zip; a
queue of five addon zips would read 2 GiB to conclude nothing happened.

So the check is made cheap first:

  - The baseline at orangefox.cpp:648 is now gated on TW_AUTO_REFLASHTWRP_VAR.
    It was only inside `#ifdef AB_OTA_UPDATER`, so users who turn auto-reflash
    off were paying 200 MiB per zip for a result nothing ever read.

  - IsRecoveryOverwritten() gets an O(1) pre-filter. The kernel's per-partition
    sectors-written counter (field 7 of /sys/class/block/<dev>/stat) is
    recorded alongside the checksums; if neither slot's counter has moved since
    the last hash, nothing wrote to recovery and the hash is skipped entirely.
    This is only a filter, never a verdict: a counter that did move still falls
    through to the full sha256 compare, so a zip that writes identical bytes
    back is still correctly reported as "not overwritten". If the counters
    cannot be read (no sysfs, unresolvable device, short stat line) the code
    hashes unconditionally, exactly as before.

    Net effect: one 200 MiB hash for the whole session instead of 400 MiB per
    zip, with identical results.

Also fixed while here:

  - Ordering. Doing the reflash after the ROM block's tail meant it ran after
    post_rom_flash_completion.sh and after the
    OF_BLOCK_OPERATIONS_AFTER_ROM_FLASH warning, so on a ROM install the user
    would be told "You must now reboot OrangeFox - immediately!" before
    recovery had actually been restored. The helper is called before that
    messaging on the ROM path.

  - The reflash path does not need its own Update_System_Details():
    Repack_Image_And_Flash() already calls it during the slot switch, and
    action.cpp calls it again after the zip loop.

  - The restore notice goes through gui_msg() like the surrounding code, rather
    than a bare gui_print(), so it is translatable. Its string is added to
    gui/theme/common/languages/en.xml, which LoadPackage() always loads as the
    base language before overlaying the selected one, so the id resolves in
    every locale and the "String resource not found" fallback warning is gone.

